Service discovery is failing across namespaces. DNS is green.
What’s your next step — and how do you confirm the root cause?”

Here’s what they expected from a senior DevOps engineer:
1. Use kubectl exec to run nslookup with the full FQDN: svc.namespace.svc.cluster.local
2. Check if the pod’s ServiceAccount has permission to list services in that namespace
3. Inspect RBAC roles — is there a RoleBinding for discovery?
4. Review NetworkPolicies – are they blocking intra-namespace traffic?
5. Examine CoreDNS ConfigMap – look for caching misconfigurations or recursive loops
6. Use tcpdump inside the pod to confirm UDP port 53 DNS traffic
7. Confirm EndpointSlices and actual backend pod readiness

💡 The painful lesson?

Everyone knows how DNS is supposed to work.
But very few know how it breaks in production — silently, without logs, while everything looks healthy.

Here’s what I now simulate in my mock interviews:
• Namespace-to-namespace failures with layered RBAC blocks
• Debugging CoreDNS when the plugin chain is misordered
• Live fire drills: service is healthy, traffic fails, what now?
• RCA workflows that get you promoted, not panicked
